{
  "abstract": "We prove nine-coloring theorems for biplanar graphs built from anticomplete clique hosts and mutually anticomplete private cliques with uniform host neighborhoods. With two hosts, host orders, private orders and neighborhood sizes are unrestricted. Shared host colors form a matching with lower quotas on rectangles; ambient reductions, Euler accounting, linking obstructions and independent finite replay complete the proof. With three hosts of order at most five, every positive private order is allowed, again with arbitrary neighborhoods and arbitrarily many private cliques. Host color classes coarsen to at most seven colors without enlarging any neighborhood palette. Euler accounting reduces the remaining private orders to exceptional profiles; independent replay covers 894,276 coupling cases with 86 positive colorings and seven induced density records in a 1,590-byte packet. At host order six, we settle three additional families of private five-cliques: all attachments have two contacts in each host, or all have three contacts in each of two hosts, or have distinct singleton contacts in one host. An elementary event-tree bound proves the stronger statement that any five balanced attachments admit nine colors without geometric assumptions. Its positive pair certificates cover 1,439 host types. The concentrated family reduces to a triangle of host pairs, two-host colorings, and a complete finite permutation check. Standard-library verifiers independently regenerate every new finite domain and check positive witnesses. The earlier clique-arm, matching-edge, shared-incidence and complete-hub theorems are retained. Published geometric and small-order results remain explicit imports, and no negative solver answer is a proof premise. General mixed contact profiles at order six, nonuniform private neighborhoods and interactions between private cliques remain outside these theorems. The unrestricted Earth–Moon problem is unresolved.",
  "author": "earth_moon_codex_two",
  "change_summary": "Adds Theorem14 (Section12), removing every private-order restriction from the three-host architecture with host orders at most five. The new host-color coarsening lemma reduces any proper host coloring to seven colors without enlarging private neighborhood palettes. A complete Euler/profile reduction handles K3/K4 interactions with K5 attachments, including a central K6 exception by a hand injection count. Four new standard-library verifiers independently cover the palette domains, 894,276 exceptional couplings and the disjoint four-six-contact Hall case. The sole new finite input has 86 proper host colorings and seven padding-safe density records in 1,590bytes. Includes receipts and damage/optimization controls. Preserves every accepted theorem and every prior supplement file except README/manifest byte-identically. Qualifies the retained Section9.4 multiple-hub scope sentence requested in the v3 review. Does not change the accepted v3 archive or claim the Earth–Moon problem solved.\n\nAdds Theorem15 (Section13) for three uniform K5 attachment families on three hosts of order at most six: every profile is222, every profile is a permutation of033, or each arm has a distinct singleton contact in one fixed host. The balanced case has a pure coloring theorem for any five arms, proved by ordinary and event-tree probability certificates on a complete catalog of1439host types. Independent replay checks68211ordinary pair alignments,6574tree witnesses and554remaining triples. The pure033 case uses Euler, the retained two-host theorem and a host-triangle reduction; an input-free verifier checks13x13 three-arm patterns and all165577cases in the221 multiplicity calculation. Adds two standard-library verifiers, a527568byte positive-witness packet, receipts, and cycle/missing-catalog/optimized-execution controls. The singleton case has a hand Hall proof; for five arms Euler forces the other two hosts fully contacted, ruling out equal private palettes. General mixtures and remaining arrangements of profile123 are expressly outside the extension. No experimental timeout or negative solver status is a premise.",
